---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib';

// What the "Fact-checked" badge on a lesson means, and how much of the site has one so far.
const rows = await Promise.all((['concepts', 'patterns', 'studies'] as const).map(async (c) => {
  const all = await getCollection(c);
  return { label: c === 'studies' ? 'Case studies' : c === 'concepts' ? 'Concepts' : 'Patterns', total: all.length, checked: all.filter((e) => e.data.factChecked).length };
}));
---
<Layout title="How pages are fact-checked | System Design Academy" description="What the Fact-checked badge on a lesson covers, what it does not, and how much of the site has been reviewed.">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>How pages are fact-checked</h1>
    <p class="lede">A lesson with a <span class="badge badge-checked">Fact-checked</span> badge has been through an independent accuracy review on the date shown. Pages without the badge have not been reviewed yet, which is not the same as being wrong.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel">
    <h2>Progress</h2>
    <ul class="tally">
      {rows.map((r) => <li><strong>{r.checked} of {r.total}</strong> {r.label.toLowerCase()} reviewed</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel">
    <h2>What the review does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A reviewer who did not write the page reads every claim about the real world: definitions, complexity bounds, standards, vendor defaults, who said what.</li>
      <li>It runs the code samples and checks that the numbers quoted in the text, the quiz explanations and the "how to try it" steps are the ones the code prints and the simulation shows.</li>
      <li>It checks that each quiz question has one defensible answer.</li>
      <li>It checks that a toy model is described as a toy model, and that the text does not claim more than the model shows.</li>
      <li>Wrong or overstated claims are corrected on the page. A claim the reviewer cannot confirm is softened ("typically", "in many implementations") or removed.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel">
    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The reviewers are AI models working with the code and their own knowledge. A badge is not a certificate from a human expert, and it is not a guarantee that nothing is wrong.</li>
      <li>Reviewers do not always look claims up. Attributions to named people and vendor defaults (timeouts, limits, port ranges) may still rest on memory, and vendors change their defaults.</li>
      <li>The simulations use stated assumptions. Their numbers show how an idea behaves, not what a particular production system would measure.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Treat the badge as a sign that someone else has read the page critically and the numbers on it have been checked against the code. For anything you will rely on in production, confirm it against the documentation of the system you use.</p>
  </section>
  <p class="back"><a href={url('whats-new/')}>See what changed recently</a></p>
</Layout>
<style>
  .hero { padding: 2.5rem 0 1rem; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 5vw, 2.6rem); margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .lede { max-width: 62ch; }
  .panel { display: grid; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; max-width: 72ch; }
  .panel h2 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .panel ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  .tally { list-style: none; padding-left: 0 !important; }
  .back { margin: 1.5rem 0 3rem; }
  .badge-checked { text-decoration: none; color: var(--good); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 40%, var(--border)); }
</style>
